48515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1031 "Infinite Limits from Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1033 1814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7d21c9de-358a-52ec-b910-f6117a085d7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068547 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068547 #edb/ref 1071 "Is the function $y=f(x),$ plotted below, continuous at $x=0?$\n\n![](images/e3/e395863ca66a7e87fa335c56e5b6aeb1ec206893e57b155bf64e5ab07be7545c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f12b3891-021d-5ae3-a767-f9d423408ce6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068547 #edb/ref 1074 "Because there is an asymptote at $x=0,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nTherefore, the function $y=f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068548 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068548 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the points of discontinuity of the function $y=g(x),$ plotted below?\n\n![](images/6d/6d0d3c2cd072841546e66d70105436200e316e156901f8fe27dc150e96ff3da4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "514240a0-8a38-564f-ab4c-d0bacb0c5f6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068548 #edb/ref 1074 "The points of discontinuity are at $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2.5.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these three points without taking our pencil off of the page." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068549 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068549 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068549 #edb/ref 1074 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false.  The graph of the function breaks at $x=2.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068695 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186336004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068695 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 175