17] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186514550 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1045 "Determining Continuity from Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "09c8fe50-cab2-5dfd-a90a-5053ffffc1bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069367 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440261 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186069367 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440262 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186069367 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186440263 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186069367 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069367 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/02/0218a1e13a0504b20e802100f9e15877beeb2b565a49fe7cd01cf2a0cf0197ee.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is defined at $x = 2$\n- **II.** $lim_(x → 2)f(x)$ exists\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 2$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186069367 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a302c934-1e7e-57a4-882d-8cd125952f61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069367 #edb/ref 1074 "The filled point at x=2 gives f(2)=1. The left and right limits are 1 and -2, so the two-sided limit does not exist and the function is not continuous there." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186069567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069567 #edb/ref 1061 12020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3561bec4-880a-5815-bc18-84c3d62a3191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069568 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069568 #edb/ref 1061 12021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c9dde49-f9f9-5036-8e84-b7e1ced3796b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069569 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069569 #edb/ref 1061 12022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069569 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74cd2bd0-c1c2-5429-adda-d13aa18601d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069569 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069570 #edb/ref 1061 12023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069570 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64c70852-38a7-5f5e-ade8-36856f8eef67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069570 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069576 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069576 #edb/ref 1061 12404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069576 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5269250-b2f9-525d-bfaa-9f0e6033687b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069576 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069958 #edb/ref 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\n \n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069958 #edb/ref 1065 2583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069958 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fb32bea-3d77-5134-936c-bfa9f0381fdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069958 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069961 #edb/ref 1064 "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\n \n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069961 #edb/ref 1065 2640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069961 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "54ca4bef-1b8e-5315-85d4-0667910b332c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069961 #edb/ref 1067 "Continuity at a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186438703 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186438704 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186438705 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/65/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc9393f2-809c-5769-b329-186102da51c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1074 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392602 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392602 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392602 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392602 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the function that has a discontinuity at $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392602 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3d5f3e1-0f7f-56ba-9eea-f720be7a7122" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392602 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer is shown below. Notice that the function contains a break exactly at $x=1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](images/3c/3c8a198c1ec835590a3d755b964929ef9ac9a30e4268b2fdc80dacdb394899d8.png)\n\nAll the other given graphs can be plotted without taking the pencil off of the paper." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186392608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392603 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392603 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392603 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186392605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392603 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ac8479b0-0f39-5a85-82ad-a7d9ee04b2a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392604 #edb/ref 1080 "images/c9/c9ba1e63c665ad63597c822828fdf26ec0fefade083df5ea828f57a49d3aa149.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392604 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392604 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d495799e-2f9d-5146-a399-506d9d32ad84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392605 #edb/ref 1080 "images/fc/fc1d428500558ed62fbf5f7e0c13604cbc759629cedb63a8f9c2f7fc0b412480.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392605 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186392605 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c796