 continuous at $x=- \\frac{1}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=- \\frac{1}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{1}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](images/4d/4db3d16c1d05cf201e94dcbb7a8e53f82e412aa9d70a72c9766022b5d2d79466.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513496 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513497 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513500 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/14/1449fe787d2263ffd6a0d11450b34425aa4f8b0e4bd4cec0ad3a516b3b85df0c.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Use the unit-spaced tick marks to locate the listed points. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=- \\frac{9}{2}$\n- **II.** $x=-3$\n- **III.** $x=- \\frac{3}{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "304021dc-0557-5932-b360-6e01c0194b41" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=- \\frac{9}{2}.$ Because there is a jump at $x=- \\frac{9}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{9}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=- \\frac{3}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=- \\frac{3}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{3}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513512 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186068695.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513512 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "a2f5e443-dc29-5810-8b1d-6c8f8ec773d7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514550 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186068697.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514550 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "d927b53e-2990-5e2a-ad4a-8604173cab3a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514557 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514617 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514557 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514557 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d5/d51e58f4382b705fd18671b2d66d7714174c1fb0c5e7b7fd81cbe5ad4e128968.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c284724-4afb-5c0b-8e68-ac751db50f24" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514557 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph breaks at $x=-4$ and $x=1$ and $x=3.5$, meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-4$ and $x=1$ and $x=3.5$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186514602 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514561 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514561 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4b/4b15042f1927521b75608677f9b6d52581688a7170ef810ffd64e407e4b6c954.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13ca2641-4667-590e-83aa-f812bfa1d63f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186514561 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph breaks at $x=-1$ and $x=2$ and $x=3.5$, meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=2$ and $x=3.5$." #edb/ref 1759