48744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548957 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548957 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186548864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548957 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548957 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548957 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186548679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548957 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69780" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548982 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the function that is continuous at $x=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548982 #edb/ref 1074 "Of the given options, the only one where we can draw the graph of the function without taking our pencil off of the paper in a neighborhood of $x=-1$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca4279200e98b57570030ea6ecce9c04412645ff16d532f302cb13c6bf59c15e.png)\n\nAll other given options have breaks or asymptotes at $x=-1$, so it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548989 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548989 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548989 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/17/17932f6186d98bb68ff231c07ae1e2b42dbe7c391ff442db6d4e8c9da92a9a82.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548989 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548989 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph breaks at $x=2$, meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  $x=2$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549055 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549055 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549055 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549055 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9c/9c49ba7f220352a8933159a854d6e311baaaf2b6ec1e1f6fbdb78988653a157f.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. $x=-3$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549055 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-3,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is also continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"II and III only\".\n\n![](images/f9/f9c5342a872b4fd0ad2783b00d4d170b05844dc057a462832bf0eed41f50ccf4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
